{% section #step-4 step=4 title="React to project events" %}

The Appwrite MCP server supports [MCP Events](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#events), an MCP extension that lets an agent subscribe to changes in your project. Pi can subscribe to an event, such as a new user, finish its turn, and wake up when the event happens. The server pushes each event over the connection Pi already has open, so you don't need a public URL.

Pi receives events through [pi-mcp-events](https://github.com/mikekelly/pi-mcp-events), an extension that adds an `mcp_events` tool on top of pi-mcp-adapter. Install it, then restart Pi to load it:

```bash
pi install npm:@realmikekelly/pi-mcp-events
```

Ask Pi to subscribe to `users.user.created`. Replace `<PROJECT_ID>` with your project ID:

```
Subscribe with mcp_events to users.user.created on the appwrite server for project_id <PROJECT_ID>, then finish your turn and wait. Whenever an event arrives, look up that user and tell me their name and email.
```

Pi opens the subscription and goes idle. When a user signs up or you create one in the Appwrite Console, Pi wakes up without a new prompt, looks up the user, and replies with their name and email.

When you no longer need the events, ask Pi to unsubscribe:

```
Unsubscribe from users.user.created
```

The following recording shows the whole flow in a test project. Pi subscribes, wakes up when a user is created, reports the user, and then unsubscribes.

{% only_dark %}
![Pi subscribes to users.user.created, wakes up when a user is created, and unsubscribes](/images/docs/mcp/pi/events-dark.gif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Pi subscribes to users.user.created, wakes up when a user is created, and unsubscribes](/images/docs/mcp/pi/events-light.gif)
{% /only_light %}

Subscriptions last as long as the Pi session. After you restart Pi or run `/reload`, ask it to subscribe again. To see every event you can subscribe to and how delivery works, read [Events](/docs/tooling/ai/mcp-servers#events).

{% /section %}
